# ================================================================= ABSTRACT
d.replace(F('Background. A fully distalized'), '**Background. **A fully distalized maxillary arch can be achieved with headgear-assisted fixed appliances or with clear aligners and miniscrew-anchored elastics attached through a precision cut, an aligner-material hook or a bonded metal hook. These methods have not been compared in a long-term simulation.')
d.replace(F('Methods. Maxillary half-arch'), '**Methods. **Half-arch models of five patients (Open-Full-Jaw) were analysed with cervical headgear and a fixed appliance (HG; 200 gf) and with aligners and a 200 gf elastic from an infrazygomatic crest miniscrew attached through a precision cut (PC), an aligner-material hook (CAH) or a bonded metal hook (CMH), with an aligner-only control. Aligners were staged over 70 steps and the headgear acted over 140 remodelling iterations. The primary analysis compared the groups at equal first-molar distalization (0.8 mm); the end of the protocol was secondary. Sensitivity analyses tested a linear periodontal ligament and three alternative headgear force systems (Friedman and Holm-corrected paired t-tests).')
d.replace(F('Results. With the bilinear'), '**Results. **At equal first-molar distalization, HG tipped the first molar more than the aligner designs (0.94 ± 0.04 versus 0.73–0.77 mm; Holm p = 0.013) and extruded it more than PC (−0.22 versus 0.01 mm; p = 0.021). PC tipped and intruded the molar more than the hooks (p ≤ 0.004), and CAH and CMH differed by 0.01 mm or less. Every elastic design held the canine 0.19–0.25 mm further distally than aligners alone, but with a linear ligament this anchorage effect was about ten times smaller. With the headgear force through the molar\'s centre of resistance, palatal displacement of the distobuccal cusp fell from 4.71 to 1.04 mm and canine extrusion from 2.09 to 0.93 mm; matching its direction to the elastic reversed molar extrusion. End-of-protocol molar distalization was largest with HG (3.09 versus 1.29–1.39 mm) but depended on the iteration count and ligament model.')
d.replace(F('Conclusions. The aligner-material'), '**Conclusions. **In this model the two hooks were mechanically equivalent while the aligner was assumed to fit perfectly, and miniscrew-supported elastics reduced anchorage loss by an amount that depended on the ligament model. At equal distalization, cervical headgear tipped and extruded the first molar more than the aligners, but its transverse and vertical side effects depended on how the headgear force was represented.')

# ================================================================= BACKGROUND (aim)
d.replace(F('To our knowledge, no study'), 'To our knowledge, no study has compared headgear-assisted fixed appliances with aligner-based distalization, or the three ways of attaching a miniscrew-anchored elastic to an aligner, within one long-term simulation framework and across several patients. The primary aim of this study was therefore to compare three ways of attaching an IZC miniscrew-anchored elastic to a clear aligner: a precision cut, an aligner-material hook and a bonded metal hook. The secondary aim was to compare them with a conventional reference, a fixed appliance with cervical headgear. Cervical headgear was chosen as the classical extraoral, non-skeletal reference for maxillary molar distalization, which is still used in growing patients with Class II malocclusion, not as the best current fixed-appliance option; fixed appliances anchored to IZC miniscrews were not modelled. The systems were compared at the same force with a 4D FE model with staging simulation built on the anatomy of five patients. The null hypothesis was that the systems would not differ in the sagittal, vertical and transverse displacement of the first molar, canine and central incisor at equal first-molar distalization.')

# ================================================================= METHODS
d.replace(F('Long-term movement was simulated'), 'Long-term movement was simulated with the bone-remodelling scheme of Hamanaka et al. [29]. In each iteration the teeth moved elastically under the appliance load with the PDL at its original thickness; this rigid-body displacement was added to the position of the tooth, and the PDL was restored around the new position, so tooth positions and rotations accumulated in the updated geometry. For the aligner groups, each of the 70 steps used an aligner manufactured for that step\'s planned tooth positions. The planned positions were defined cumulatively from the initial (step 0) geometry, as in a set-up made at the start of treatment, and were not updated to the positions actually reached, so any shortfall carried over into the activation of the next aligner. Before each of the two PDL iterations of a step, the aligner was fitted rigidly (least squares) from the planned to the actual crown positions, simulating wear-in. The socket of each tooth in the aligner was displaced so that the crown centre landed exactly on its planned position. This displacement was delivered as a blend of bodily translation and tipping about a point on the long axis at 0.83 of the distance from the crown tip to the apex (the middle of the apical third), with the tipping angle scaled by a blend parameter of 0.40 (0, pure translation; 1, full tipping). The value 0.40 was calibrated once against the control group of the source study [26] and was not tuned to any group of this study; the absolute magnitudes of movement are therefore not independent predictions, and the study\'s claims are comparative.')
p_plan = F('The aligners followed the V-pattern')
d.replace(p_plan, d.text(p_plan) + ' The resulting planned movement at step 70, including the change in crown angulation produced by the tipping blend, is given in Table 2 (for each patient in Additional file 2). No overcorrection was programmed, so that every aligner group and patient followed the same plan.')
T2 = [['Tooth', 'Active steps', 'Planned crown-centre movement (mm)', 'Mesiodistal crown tip (°)', 'Buccolingual crown tip (°)'],
      ['17', '1–20', '2.00–2.01 distal', '+2.4 to +3.7', '−2.6 to −0.3'], ['16', '11–30', '1.71–2.00 distal', '+3.0 to +3.9', '−0.6 to +0.8'],
      ['15', '21–40', '1.94–2.00 distal', '+3.3 to +3.8', '−1.2 to +0.6'], ['14', '31–50', '1.99–2.00 distal', '+3.5 to +4.2', '−1.2 to +0.2'],
      ['13', '41–60', '1.92–2.00 distal', '+2.7 to +3.4', '0.0 to +1.1'], ['12', '51–70', '0.75–2.29 palatal', '−0.1 to 0.0', '−3.2 to −1.2'],
      ['11', '51–70', '0.75–2.29 palatal', '−0.1 to 0.0', '−3.8 to −1.1']]
d.insert_after(p_plan, [cap('**Table 2 **Planned tooth movement at aligner step 70 (range across the five patients)'),
    d.make_table(tmpl, T2, [1000, 1300, 2526, 2100, 2100]),
    note('Movement along the fourth-order arch form (teeth 17–13) or palatally in the occlusal plane (incisors), shortened where the collision-free plan required it. Crown tip is the change in long-axis angulation produced by the 0.40 tipping blend: mesiodistal positive, distal crown tip; buccolingual positive, buccal crown tip (negative for the incisors, palatal crown tip). No overcorrection was programmed.')])

p_hg = F('Fixed appliance with cervical headgear (HG). Ceramic')
d.replace(p_hg, '**Fixed appliance with cervical headgear (HG). **Ceramic twin brackets with an MBT prescription (0.022-inch slot; torque +17° for 11, +10° for 12, 0° for 13 and −7° for 14 and 15), read from a computer-aided design library, were bonded to teeth 15–11 at the facial-axis point, and tubes were added on 16 and 17 (torque −14°, offset 10°) with a 0.045-inch headgear tube on 16. A passive 0.019 × 0.025-inch stainless steel archwire (elastic modulus 210 GPa) was modelled as co-rotational beams that could slide through every bracket and tube, with the slot clearance (±0.038 mm) and torque play (±7.3°), penalty contact beyond them (10,000 N/mm) and Coulomb friction of 0.15 [8, 30]. Because the brackets were placed on the unaligned teeth, the wire was straight in each slot and bent between slots, so a bracket sliding distally moved onto wire of a different local orientation; alignment and levelling were not simulated. A cervical headgear force of 200 gf per side acted at the headgear tube along a line fixed in space 15° below the occlusal plane [5], representing the force of the inner bow; the outer-bow moment and inner-bow expansion were not modelled. Brackets were rigid with their teeth. Movement was force-driven over 140 remodelling iterations, matching the number of PDL iterations of the aligner groups; this number has no clinical time meaning.')

p_izc = F('In the three aligner groups the miniscrew head')
d.replace(p_izc, d.text(p_izc) + ' As a result, the elastic pulled 19.7–26.3° above the occlusal plane towards the precision cut (about 13° towards the hook tips in patient 8), whereas the headgear pulled 15° below it (Fig. 2).')
d.insert_after(p_izc, [d.new_figure(fig_tmpl, os.path.join(SP, 'figs', 'Fig_line_of_action_P8.png')),
    legend('**Fig. 2 Line of action of the 200 gf force in each group.** Patient 8, a lateral and b occlusal view. HG: force at the headgear tube of 16, 15° below the occlusal plane (OP; solid arrow, main model) and along the direction of the miniscrew elastic, 25° above it (dashed arrow, sensitivity analysis); ×, centroid of 16, used as its centre of resistance. PC, CAH and CMH: elastic from the miniscrew head (square) to the precision cut, the tip of the aligner-material hook or the tip of the metal hook (circle). In the five patients the elastic to the precision cut ran 19.7–26.3° above the occlusal plane. HG, fixed appliance with cervical headgear; PC, precision cut; CAH, aligner-material hook; CMH, bonded metal hook.')])

d.replace(F('Because one remodelling iteration is not calibrated'), '**Comparison points. **Because one remodelling iteration has no calibrated clinical duration, and the headgear group is force-driven whereas the aligner groups follow a staging plan, the groups cannot be compared at equal time. The primary analysis therefore compared them at equal outcome: when the mesiobuccal cusp of the first molar had moved 0.8 mm distally (linear interpolation between steps). This point lies within the molar phase of the aligner plan, before any premolar, canine or incisor is activated, so it compares the side effects of molar distalization and the reciprocal anchorage loss; anterior values at this point measure anchorage loss, not planned retraction. The threshold of 0.8 mm (40% of the planned 2.0 mm) was tested from 0.4 to 1.2 mm, and the groups were also compared along the whole distalization path. The secondary, exploratory analysis compared the groups at the end of the protocol (aligner step 70; headgear iteration 140), the only point at which the planned anterior retraction of the aligner groups is complete, but whose headgear values depend on the arbitrary number of iterations. Displacements per millimetre of first-molar distalization are reported descriptively in Additional file 2.')
d.replace(F('The patient was the unit of analysis'), 'The patient was the unit of analysis (n = 5). The model is deterministic, so variation between results reflects anatomical differences between patients. At each comparison point the four appliance groups were compared with the Friedman test, followed by pairwise paired t-tests with Holm correction over the six pairs. The anchorage effect of the elastics was tested separately by comparing each elastic design with the aligner-only control (paired t-tests, Holm correction over the three contrasts). Given the sample size the pairwise tests were considered exploratory, and the number of patients in which each difference had the same sign is reported alongside the p-values. Analyses were carried out in Python, with a significance level of 0.05.')
p_sens = F('Two sensitivity analyses were run on all five patients.')
d.replace(p_sens, 'Three sensitivity analyses were run on all five patients: all five groups with a linear PDL (elastic modulus 0.68 MPa), as used in static FE studies; the aligner groups with the original plan of the source protocol without interproximal contact; and the headgear group with three alternative force systems (Fig. 2). These were the force at the tube along the elastic direction of each patient (19.7–26.3° above the occlusal plane), the cervical force with its line of action through the centroid of 16 (an outer bow adjusted for bodily movement, with no moment), and the main force system with every bracket and tube seated on one flat, passive archwire (a levelled set-up). On patient 8, the force (150 and 250 gf), miniscrew height (6 mm apical to the crest), hook arm length (6 mm), aligner-material arm width (1.5 and 3.0 mm), headgear angle (0° and 30° below the occlusal plane), archwire coupling (bonded) and wire–slot friction (0 and 0.30) were varied one at a time, together with the interproximal (250 and 1000 N/mm) and wire–slot (5000 and 20,000 N/mm) contact penalties. Adjacent-tooth interpenetration was checked at the end of every run, and the PDL iteration cap was raised from 35 to 60 for patient 5, in whom some iterations of steps 12–20 reached the cap.')

d.replace(F('The staging problem was solved'), 'The staging problem was solved with a purpose-written FE code (rigid-tooth projection, active-set contact and a secant bilinear PDL, with assembly and sparse direct factorization on a graphics processor), which had been verified against FEBio 4 [32] and for mesh convergence before this study (Additional file 2). After the fixed-appliance components were added, the unchanged platform reproduced its reference run exactly, and the archwire model passed rigid-body and small-deformation checks.')

# ================================================================= RESULTS
p_first = F('All 25 runs (five groups')
d.replace(p_first, 'All runs completed. The 0.8-mm distalization of the first molar used for the primary comparison was reached on average at step 18.6 (PC), 18.9 (CAH and CMH) and 21.2 (control), and at the equivalent of step 4.7 of the headgear protocol (about nine remodelling iterations); in the aligner groups this was within the molar phase of the plan, before any premolar, canine or incisor had been activated. Mean displacements at equal molar distalization are given in Table 3 and Fig. 3 and at the end of the protocol in Table 4 and Fig. 5; values for every patient and all pairwise comparisons are given in Additional file 1, and effect sizes, per-patient profiles, pairwise matrices and the sensitivity of the matched comparison in Additional file 2.')

# old blocks
