# sensitivity: linear PDL table with control, headgear variants
p203 = F('The constitutive model of the PDL had a large effect')
d.replace(p203, 'With the linear PDL (Table 5), first-molar crown distalization with headgear fell from 3.09 to 1.26 ± 0.38 mm and no longer differed from the aligner designs (0.94–0.95 mm; Friedman p = 0.78), nor did incisor retraction (p = 0.56). The aligner designs then extruded the incisal edge by 0.76–0.81 mm, whereas headgear did not (p ≤ 0.004, all patients). Headgear again displaced the distobuccal cusp palatally (−0.87 ± 0.19 mm; p < 0.001), extruded the canine (0.22 ± 0.07 mm) and tipped the molar more (1.47 versus 0.87 mm). The precision cut and the hooks differed by 0.09 mm or less, and the two hooks by 0.013 mm or less in any patient.')
cap_T4 = F('Table 4 Sensitivity analysis'); tbl_T4 = cap_T4.getnext(); note_T4 = tbl_T4.getnext()
for e in (cap_T4, note_T4): d.mark_par_deleted(e)
d.mark_tbl(tbl_T4, 'del')
rows = [['Outcome (mm)', 'HG', 'PC', 'CAH', 'CMH', 'Control', 'p']]
for m, lab in ROWS:
    V = {g: val(g, m, 'final') for g in G_LIN}
    fr, _ = compare({g: V[g] for g in G_LIN[:4]}, G_LIN[:4])
    rows.append([lab] + [ms(V[g]) for g in G_LIN] + [f'{fr.pvalue:.3f}'])
anchor = note_T4 if d.track else p203
plin = body('With the linear PDL the aligner-only control distalized the first molar crown by 0.88 ± 0.33 mm, close to the elastic designs (0.94–0.95 mm). The elastic designs still held the canine further distally than the control (by 0.03–0.06 mm; 13 of 15 patient-level comparisons) and kept the incisal edge further palatal (by 0.12–0.14 mm) and less extruded (by 0.08–0.14 mm) in every patient (Holm p ≤ 0.022), but these anchorage effects were about one tenth of those with the bilinear PDL.')
d.insert_after(anchor, [cap('**Table 5 **Sensitivity analysis: tooth displacement with a linear PDL (0.68 MPa) at the end of the protocol (mean ± SD, n = 5)'),
    d.make_table(tmpl, rows, [2526, 1100, 1100, 1100, 1100, 1100, 1000]),
    note('Same landmarks and sign conventions as Table 4. p, Friedman test across HG, PC, CAH and CMH. HG, fixed appliance with cervical headgear; PC, aligner with precision cut; CAH, aligner with aligner-material hook; CMH, aligner with bonded metal hook; Control, aligner without elastics; MB, mesiobuccal; DB, distobuccal.'),
    plin])
hv = body('**Headgear force systems. **Pointing the headgear force along the elastic direction turned the extrusion of the first molar into intrusion (1.06 ± 0.25 mm at the end of the protocol) and reduced canine extrusion from 2.09 to 1.36 mm, but nearly doubled distal crown tipping of the molar (2.28 to 4.13 mm). Passing the cervical force through the centroid of the first molar reduced the palatal displacement of the distobuccal cusp from 4.71 to 1.04 ± 0.44 mm and canine extrusion to 0.93 ± 0.13 mm, and at equal distalization it removed the extra molar tipping (0.78 ± 0.06 mm). The levelled set-up changed the results little. At equal distalization, the headgear-versus-precision-cut differences in molar extrusion, palatal molar displacement and canine distalization kept their sign in at least four of five patients under every variant, whereas the canine vertical difference reversed with the matched direction (Additional file 2, Tables S13 and S14).')
d.insert_after(plin, [hv])

d.replace(F('With the original plan of the source protocol'), 'With the original plan of the source protocol, the aligner designs retained the direction of every between-group difference in all five patients (210 of 210 patient-level comparisons). On patient 8 (Additional file 2), the force magnitude, miniscrew height, arm length, arm width, headgear angle, archwire coupling and friction changed the amount of movement but not the order of the groups for first-molar distalization, and the two hook designs remained within 0.08 mm of each other at every crown and root point.')

# ================================================================= DISCUSSION
d.replace(F('This study compared headgear-assisted fixed appliances'), 'This study compared three methods of attaching a miniscrew-anchored elastic to a clear aligner with each other and with a headgear-assisted fixed appliance, within a single 4D FE framework across five patients. Because force-driven headgear and staged aligners cannot be compared at equal time, the groups were compared primarily at equal first-molar distalization. Four findings stand out. Firstly, an aligner-material hook and a bonded metal hook of the same geometry behaved almost identically, in a model that assumes a perfectly fitting aligner. Secondly, every elastic design reduced anterior anchorage loss compared with aligners alone, but the size of this effect depended strongly on the PDL model. Thirdly, at equal molar distalization the cervical headgear tipped and extruded the first molar more than the aligner designs, whereas its large transverse and vertical side effects at the end of the protocol depended on how the headgear force was represented. Finally, attaching the elastic through a precision cut rather than a hook slightly altered the type of molar and canine movement.')
p243 = F('The findings of the headgear group are consistent')
d.replace(p243, 'The findings of the headgear group are consistent with a cervical line of force passing below the centre of resistance of the first molar, which FE studies associate with distal crown tipping and extrusion [5–7]; clinically, cervical headgear extruded the first molar by about 1 mm [1]. At equal distalization the molar was extruded and tipped more than with any aligner design, and its root apex moved mesially. The sensitivity analyses show which parts of this pattern belong to the force system. With the line of action through the centroid of the first molar, as an adjusted outer bow would direct it, the extra tipping disappeared, and at the end of the protocol the palatal displacement of the distobuccal cusp fell from 4.71 to 1.04 mm and canine extrusion from 2.09 to 0.93 mm. The mesial-out rotation of the molar reported with headgear [5] therefore came mainly from the moment of a force at the buccal tube, which clinicians counter by adjusting the outer bow and expanding the inner bow.')
d.insert_after(p243, [body('The force vectors also differed: the elastic pulled 19.7–26.3° above the occlusal plane and the cervical headgear 15° below it. Turned to the elastic direction, the headgear intruded the molar by 1.06 mm instead of extruding it and extruded the canine less, but tipped the molar nearly twice as much. The vertical differences between headgear and aligners therefore reflect the cervical line of force rather than the appliance type, and apply only to a cervical headgear; a combined headgear would lie between the two directions tested. The differences in molar tipping, root-apex movement, transverse movement and canine position at equal distalization kept their sign in four or five of five patients with the matched direction.')])
d.replace(F('In the headgear group the force applied to the molar also moved'), 'In the headgear group the archwire transmitted the force to the anterior teeth through slot friction; with a frictionless wire the molar moved distally largely on its own. With the bilinear PDL the incisors were retracted by more than 3 mm but tipped lingually and extruded, as in the FE analysis of total distalization by Kawamura et al., where this arose from archwire–slot clearance and archwire deflection [8], and in clinical studies of IZC-supported distalization [3, 4]. On a levelled set-up the canine extrusion and palatal molar displacement changed little, so they were not caused by the bends of the unlevelled wire. With the bilinear PDL every aligner design intruded the canine and the incisors, consistent with the intrusive component of an elastic from a miniscrew high in the IZC region [8, 11, 15, 19], but with the linear PDL the aligners extruded the incisors by about 0.8 mm, so the vertical comparison cannot be settled by this model alone.')
d.replace(F('The dependence on the PDL model has a mechanical explanation.'), 'The dependence on the PDL model has a mechanical explanation. The headgear group is force-driven: a constant 200 gf acts on the molar in every iteration, so the movement per iteration is inversely related to the stiffness of the ligament. The bilinear PDL of the source protocol is very compliant (0.05 MPa below 7.5% strain), whereas the linear PDL (0.68 MPa) is several times stiffer, and molar distalization with headgear fell from 3.09 to 1.26 mm. The aligner groups are displacement-driven: the aligner imposes a planned movement at every step, so their molar distalization changed less (from 1.29–1.39 to 0.94–0.95 mm). The same mechanism reduced the anchorage effect of the elastics: with the stiffer ligament, the 2 N elastic is small relative to the contact forces between aligner and teeth, and the teeth follow the aligner more closely. Neither PDL model has been calibrated against clinical treatment, so the amount of headgear distalization relative to staged aligners and the size of the anchorage effect are the least certain results of this study.')
d.replace(F('The larger distalization with headgear should also be interpreted'), 'The larger end-of-protocol distalization with headgear does not indicate greater clinical efficiency, because a remodelling iteration is not calibrated to clinical time and the 140 iterations were chosen only to match the number of PDL iterations of the aligner groups. The headgear reached the 0.8-mm matched distalization after about nine iterations, whereas the aligner groups needed about 19 steps, which is why the comparison at equal distalization was the primary analysis. This comparison also has a limit: it falls before any anterior tooth is activated in the aligner plan, so its anterior values measure anchorage loss rather than planned retraction, and some differences, such as molar tipping, reversed at small amounts of distalization (Fig. 4). Clinically, meta-analytic evidence shows more molar distalization and tipping and more anchorage loss with conventional appliances than with aligners [2], which agrees in direction with the main results.')
d.replace(F('Comparisons of precision cuts with hooks have reached'), 'Earlier FE studies found that a precision cut reinforced anchorage better than a button bonded to the canine [17, 18], that aligner deformation made the button more effective [19], or that the traction site matters more than the method [20]. Here the precision cut produced slightly more molar and canine distalization, but also more molar tipping, molar intrusion and buccal movement of the distobuccal cusp, and less incisor intrusion. With a precision cut the elastic loads the aligner, which distributes the load to all crowns as it deforms; with either hook the load enters at the canine, as was found in vitro [22]. Domingos et al. reported molar extrusion with precision cuts and vertical attachments [21], perhaps because of their attachment design and single-step analysis. These differences were consistent in sign but small (at most about 0.5 mm with the bilinear PDL and 0.09 mm with the linear PDL).')
d.replace(F('The aligner-material hook and the bonded metal hook differed on average'), 'The aligner-material hook and the bonded metal hook differed on average by 0.03 mm or less at every landmark, and by at most 0.08 mm in any patient or sensitivity run. The force-transfer analysis (Additional file 2) explains why: the metal hook pulled the canine distally with the full elastic force (about 1.7 N), but the aligner, which still imposed the planned positions, then carried correspondingly less distal load to the canine, so the net load on the canine differed by only 0.4–0.9 N between the two hooks and on every other tooth by less than 0.1 N. In other words, the aligner contact absorbed the extra force delivered by the metal hook. This equivalence depends on the assumptions of the model: the aligner fitted perfectly, behaved linear-elastically without stress relaxation and was never displaced. The features that distinguish the two hooks clinically, such as loss of aligner retention, lift-off of the aligner at the canine, deformation, fatigue or fracture of an aligner-material hook, and debonding of the button, were not modelled. The results therefore show that the two designs load the dentition alike while the aligner is fully seated, not that they are clinically interchangeable.')
d.replace(F('All three elastic designs reduced anterior anchorage loss'), 'All three elastic designs reduced anterior anchorage loss compared with the aligner-only control. At equal molar distalization they held the canine 0.19–0.25 mm further distally and the incisal edge 0.19–0.23 mm further palatally in every patient, and at the end of the protocol they increased canine distalization and incisor retraction and converted incisor extrusion into intrusion. The direction of these effects was the same with the original staging plan and with the linear PDL, and it agrees with previous FE studies of miniscrew-supported aligner distalization [23–27]. Their size, however, depended strongly on the PDL model: with the linear PDL the elastics added only 0.03–0.06 mm to canine distalization and 0.12–0.14 mm to incisor retraction, about one tenth of the effect with the bilinear PDL.')
d.replace(F('The aligner designs distalized the first molar crown by about'), 'The aligner designs distalized the first molar crown by about 1.3–1.4 mm of a planned 2.0 mm and tipped it distally. Clinical studies report about 2 mm of first-molar distalization with aligners, with variable tipping [12–14, 33, 34], and only about 1 mm of a prescribed 3 mm with IZC anchorage [15]. Because the model is calibrated to a previous simulation, these absolute values are not predictions, but their order of magnitude is compatible with the clinical range.')
p259 = F('The main strength of this study')
d.replace(p259, d.text(p259).replace('with sensitivity analyses that separate robust from model-dependent results', 'with sensitivity analyses that show which results depend on the assumptions of the model'))
d.replace(F('The study has several limitations.'), 'The study has several limitations. First, the PDL model strongly affected the amount of headgear distalization and the size of the anchorage effect, and neither PDL model has been calibrated against clinical treatment. Second, the headgear was a constant force at the tube on a line fixed in space, without outer bow, inner-bow expansion, force decay or intermittent wear; its transverse and vertical side effects depended on this representation, and its force direction differed from that of the elastics. Third, alignment and levelling were not simulated. Fourth, only the right half-arch was modelled with a symmetry constraint, so asymmetry, midline effects and bilateral width control, such as by a transpalatal arch, could not be represented. Fifth, teeth and bone were rigid, and remodelling was represented by restoring the PDL rather than by a biological model. Sixth, the aligner fitted perfectly, was linear-elastic without stress relaxation and was never displaced, and hook deformation and loss of retention were not modelled. Seventh, socket kinematics were calibrated against a previous simulation, no overcorrection was programmed, and a remodelling iteration is not calibrated to clinical time. Eighth, the matched comparison precedes activation of the anterior teeth, and some of its differences depended on the threshold. Finally, the single-parameter variations were run on one patient, the pairwise tests with five patients are exploratory, and the results have not been validated against clinical outcomes.')
d.replace(F('Within the limitations of this four-dimensional'), 'Within the limitations of this four-dimensional finite element analysis, a bonded metal hook and an aligner-material hook of the same geometry produced practically identical tooth movement while the aligner was assumed to fit perfectly; whether they behave alike clinically, where aligner retention and hook deformation matter, remains to be tested. Miniscrew-supported elastics reduced anterior anchorage loss with every attachment design, consistently in direction but by an amount that depended strongly on the PDL model. At equal first-molar distalization, a cervical headgear acting at the molar tube tipped and extruded the first molar more than the aligner designs; its palatal molar displacement and canine extrusion were largely removed when the force passed through the centre of resistance of the molar, and its vertical effects reversed when its direction was matched to the miniscrew elastic. Attaching the elastic through a precision cut rather than a hook changed molar tipping and intrusion by small amounts. Comparisons between force-driven and staged appliances in 4D FE models should be made at equal tooth movement and should report their sensitivity to the PDL model and to the force system.')

